1
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:40,040
 1190. While the West was busy crusading and building cathedrals, Asia was being overrun by Mongol horsemen under Genghis Khan.

2
00:01:47,870 --> 00:01:56,160
 Come here!

3
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:12,490
 First dung mixed with dried grass, the only fuel they were able to find.

4
00:02:40,440 --> 00:03:08,890
 Have you seen the Tajula?

5
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:13,530
 Find that camp just three days away.

6
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:15,570
 Thanks, my friends.

7
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:19,220
 Hey, boys, let's kill these traders.

8
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:42,550
 Their brains in!

9
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:53,160
 And that, my friends, is how Temujin, with his handful of men, began the lifetime of conquest that would one day make him Genghis Khan, master of one of history's greatest empires.

10
00:03:53,160 --> 00:04:30,330
 He claimed Khan in 1200. In 1207, he conquered Mongolia and Siberia. In 1209, Tibet. The invasion of China, 1211. Kazakhstan followed in 1218. 1220, the war against Khorasan, which had offended the Mongols and wasn't even successful.

11
00:04:30,330 --> 00:04:35,620
 Genghis Khan mustered 200,000 horsemen and supplemented them by arming local Muslims.

12
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:42,860
 At the same time, in 1214, at the Battle of Bouvines, French horsemen numbered only 1,000.

13
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:52,830
 I think there was no greater pleasure in life than to chop up one's enemies,

14
00:04:53,110 --> 00:04:55,430
 chase them from their homes, lay claim to their goods,

15
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:00,000
 reduce their loved ones to tears, and make off with their wives and daughters.

16
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:05,240
 And yet, that was the creed of Genghis Khan.

17
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:17,110
 When he died at 65, some say he fell from his horse, his empire was immense.

18
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:21,420
 But it would grow still larger, added to by sons and grandsons.

19
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:22,800
 The most famous was Kubla.

20
00:05:23,230 --> 00:05:26,510
 On his deathbed, Genghis gave this little boy his special blessing.

21
00:05:29,270 --> 00:05:34,050
 And if the Pope consents to send me six wise men of the Christian faith,

22
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:35,490
 knowledgeable in the seven arts,

23
00:05:35,550 --> 00:05:38,050
 and if they can debate and prove clearly to us pagans

24
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:39,950
 that faith in Christ is the best faith,

25
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:42,310
 and that all others are false and evil,

26
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:45,500
 then I, the great Khan, and all those in my power,

27
00:05:45,870 --> 00:05:48,620
 will become Christians and liegemen of your church.

28
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:54,780
 That is the message the great Kubla Khan sent with the Polo brothers,

29
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:58,680
 two wealthy Italian merchants, when they left his court and headed home.

30
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:03,880
 Two years later, they returned, bringing with them their son and nephew, Marco.

31
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:04,480
 Hey, how's it going?

32
00:06:06,510 --> 00:06:11,170
 We're going to a chapango and cafe, but first we'll put in an Acra.

33
00:06:11,470 --> 00:06:13,520
 We've got to see Pope Gregory at Plaisance.

34
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:18,620
 And here's Acra, my friends, I'm so sorry, but we'll have to part.

35
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:23,060
 Muscles like these be real handy on a ship.

36
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:24,700
 Yeah, and how's about some brains? He's smart.

37
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:28,010
 Hold on a bit, boys.

38
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:33,240
 Father, would it be okay to bring another two sturdy boys with us?

39
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:34,440
 They might just give us a hand.

40
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:37,160
 What a bright idea, Marco. Your friends can come along.

41
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:38,680
 Thanks, Pop. I'm going to tell them.

42
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:41,780
 You guys, hey, listen, you're coming!

43
00:06:42,040 --> 00:07:03,700
 Father, the great Khan, to whose court we are traveling,

44
00:07:03,880 --> 00:07:06,420
 respectfully asks that emissaries be sent out by his holiness

45
00:07:06,420 --> 00:07:08,680
 to bear him news of the Western lands and Christianity.

46
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:16,010
 This mark of respect may predispose him favorably to our cause.

47
00:07:16,510 --> 00:07:18,380
 I've got just the thing for you, my boy.

48
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:19,640
 But, Holy Father...

49
00:07:19,640 --> 00:07:21,310
 Yes, yes, yes, the holy oil.

50
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:26,350
 But we will also need a few ambassadors like these venerable brothers.

51
00:07:27,230 --> 00:07:27,510
 Huh?

52
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:29,230
 How true. Take him along, those two.

53
00:07:29,230 --> 00:07:32,670
 But wouldn't we be of more use to by remaining here with you, Holy Father?

54
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:34,850
 They fine with you?

55
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:38,380
 Well, I had actually hoped to have at the very least six.

56
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:42,160
 Oh, nope. Better forget about six. I can't spare six, no.

57
00:07:42,590 --> 00:07:44,040
 Well then, how about five?

58
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:44,760
 No, no.

59
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:45,540
 Or perhaps four?

60
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:46,640
 I said no.

61
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:48,520
 Well, can I count on two then?

62
00:07:48,890 --> 00:07:50,610
 That's what I seem to remember saying.

63
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,310
 I repeat that Brother Nicholas of Vincenza and Brother William of Tripoli...

64
00:07:57,310 --> 00:07:59,110
 What the... What's he up to now?

65
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:01,240
 Brother William...

66
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:05,170
 Brother William...

67
00:08:05,170 --> 00:08:07,780
 Come out wherever you are.

68
00:08:10,180 --> 00:08:15,050
 Please come down immediately. You know that ascensions are forbidden without my permission.

69
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:16,750
 Ah-choo!

70
00:08:17,470 --> 00:08:19,070
 Father God in heaven, protect me.

71
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:32,040
 Listen, someone's coming.

72
00:08:35,970 --> 00:08:38,710
 Sent from Cairo, coming to invade us?

73
00:08:38,710 --> 00:08:40,350
 Not at all, just in from Venice, see?

74
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:49,780
 How about a nice purse of gold?

75
00:08:51,820 --> 00:08:55,240
 I should be settled for one crummy little bag.

76
00:08:55,300 --> 00:08:56,640
 We might as well take all I got, eh?

77
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,010
 And besides, who's here to stop us from capturing those ninnies and selling them as slaves?

78
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:03,420
 Let's gift wrap these strangers.

79
00:09:13,930 --> 00:09:25,210
 Yes, sir, a brilliant mission.

80
00:09:25,430 --> 00:09:27,100
 If we can stay alive, we'd prefer to.

81
00:09:27,310 --> 00:09:28,750
 But the orders of the Holy Father...

82
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:31,330
 Now, don't take offense, please, but we have to admit we're poor travelers.

83
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:34,390
 If you insist, go ahead, my friend.

84
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,120
 I will pray for you.

85
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:39,460
 Wait, God keep you safe.

86
00:09:39,750 --> 00:09:40,790
 And you as well, amen.

87
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:54,110
 Our friend's caravan pushed on into the Middle East.

88
00:09:54,370 --> 00:09:54,830
 One day,

89
00:09:55,340 --> 00:09:59,840
 Look, it's Noah's Ark.

90
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:03,010
 Come now, we really should be moving along.

91
00:10:12,630 --> 00:10:17,210
 And they'll be able to see just what I'm made of.

92
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:25,250
 And another day in Mosul.

93
00:10:25,630 --> 00:10:28,120
 One moment, what's that dark liquid in those jars?

94
00:10:29,360 --> 00:10:37,120
 Their next stop was Baghdad.

95
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:40,690
 Catch him, we'll show him a thing or two or three or four or five.

96
00:10:40,690 --> 00:10:43,930
 From there, a small sailboat took them to the port of Hormuz on the Persian Gulf.

97
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:49,080
 Want it for sure.

98
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:52,410
 I would be happy to provide you with this magnificent boat, sirs.

99
00:10:52,610 --> 00:10:54,150
 Safety guaranteed, naturally.

100
00:10:55,020 --> 00:10:56,980
 Can we be sure that there won't be a leak?

101
00:11:01,990 --> 00:11:04,670
 Ash cans.

102
00:11:05,370 --> 00:11:08,430
 You wouldn't have set your eyes on any rabble like that.

103
00:11:08,470 --> 00:11:09,690
 Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no.

104
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:17,750
 If someone asks about us, say we've gone to sea.

105
00:11:17,830 --> 00:11:33,850
 Ah, just be some side. They're sitting away, see?

106
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:36,830
 That goose is cooked. Let's go!

107
00:11:41,510 --> 00:11:53,400
 Friends headed up to Kurasan.

108
00:11:57,050 --> 00:12:01,480
 Then, an unexpected gate bushed from them.

109
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:25,320
 Many setbacks out of the way, our friends were free to pursue their journey.

110
00:12:26,020 --> 00:12:27,840
 All the way to the foothills of the Hindu Kush.

111
00:12:29,030 --> 00:12:33,990
 Their crossing was difficult, very difficult, and dangerous.

112
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:40,630
 But even worse was the Gobi Desert, with its infernal sandstorms.

113
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:11,590
 Safely across the desert, Polo and his band reach the city of Shamu, renowned for its hospitality.

114
00:13:29,090 --> 00:13:33,450
 Even the fiercest of men are sometimes in need of rest.

115
00:13:33,450 --> 00:13:46,170
 Bottom comes, time to change pastures.

116
00:13:46,380 --> 00:13:50,380
 Driving their flocks in front of them, the Mongol tribesmen come down from their high plateaus.

117
00:13:56,940 --> 00:14:07,520
 Look like...

118
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:11,200
 And that one, isn't he our king-sized friend?

119
00:14:11,450 --> 00:14:13,010
 A strange bunch, those Mongols.

120
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:33,650
 Camp settles into a routine.

121
00:14:45,320 --> 00:14:50,170
 Who's boss, you puny hunk of livestock?

122
00:14:57,580 --> 00:15:06,010
 While the hunters are out chasing wolves, back at camp, life goes on normally.

123
00:15:45,180 --> 00:16:12,250
 There, do you know the law?

124
00:16:14,860 --> 00:16:20,650
 If a thief was able to pay back nine times the amount he stole, he was let off.

125
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:28,950
 Say there, you've got nothing else to give us?

126
00:16:29,070 --> 00:16:29,740
 Nothing left.

127
00:16:29,740 --> 00:16:36,700
 If not, he was good for seven lashes, or seventeen, or thirty-seven, up to a hundred and seven, depending on the severity of his crime.

128
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:46,760
 And should anyone make off with a horse, it was off with his life, his body sliced down the middle with a sword.

129
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:57,730
 Guys behind.

130
00:16:58,380 --> 00:16:59,400
 That's some funny.

131
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:22,250
 ...of your visit, appointed me as your escort and protector in case of need.

132
00:17:22,250 --> 00:17:28,060
 Show us the way, my good friends, to the palace of the great Kubla!

133
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:48,050
 He followed the winding Yellow River, all the way to Peking, their destination.

134
00:17:48,050 --> 00:17:50,170
 Apollo's journey had lasted nearly four years.

135
00:17:50,170 --> 00:17:52,310
 Greetings!

136
00:17:52,310 --> 00:17:56,640
 Greetings!

137
00:17:56,640 --> 00:18:00,800
 The emperor was the most powerful sovereign to have existed in all of recorded history.

138
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:05,050
 They say he was neither short nor tall, a stocky fellow but well-built for all that.

139
00:18:05,050 --> 00:18:08,050
 A complexion of peaches and cream, dark eyes, noble nose.

140
00:18:08,050 --> 00:18:12,290
 He had a total of four wives, as well as hundreds of concubines.

141
00:18:13,070 --> 00:18:20,270
 The Great Kubla also kept a collection of pure white mares, each one spotless, and there were 10,000 of them.

142
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:26,300
 The Sovereign himself, as well as his sons, drank these mares' milk, which was forbidden to everyone else.

143
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:35,170
 Son and humans are heavenly sons.

144
00:18:35,530 --> 00:18:40,030
 Today our almighty emperor will receive visits from strange travelers from far away.

145
00:18:42,790 --> 00:18:45,530
 All right, time to set out for work. Let's move it.

146
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:55,620
 Goodbye. May I try it?

147
00:18:55,700 --> 00:18:56,120
 Why, sure.

148
00:18:57,260 --> 00:18:58,880
 Is there anything of my size?

149
00:18:59,020 --> 00:19:00,520
 Yes, honorable stranger. One second.

150
00:19:02,590 --> 00:19:16,630
 perfect uh me i only have one on me just fine sir i want the spiffiest tunic of god

151
00:19:16,630 --> 00:19:26,480
 your most humble service spend it honorable sir like a morning star how much do you want four

152
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:36,890
 gold pieces oh might as well take five this mission will permit you to prove yourselves

153
00:19:36,890 --> 00:19:45,620
 hope you'll measure up and so our friends pay a visit to the general who is besieging the city

154
00:19:45,620 --> 00:19:55,160
 of chang fu just look at those ramparts noble strangers all our attacks are of no avail my

155
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:56,420
 My army is battle-weary.

156
00:19:56,800 --> 00:19:58,820
 I myself am about ready to pack up and leave.

157
00:20:01,840 --> 00:20:05,550
 Seems to me he might try and use a couple of those catapults I was talking about.

158
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:08,320
 Great.

159
00:20:09,110 --> 00:20:12,110
 Now Marco just happened to be a whiz at picking up tartar ways,

160
00:20:12,290 --> 00:20:14,030
 their language, both spoken and written.

161
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:16,670
 At the same time, he was a very prudent man.

162
00:20:17,430 --> 00:20:21,550
 Upon his return, Marco told the Emperor all about the new and exciting things he had seen.

163
00:20:22,490 --> 00:20:24,680
 His stories were always witty and pleasing,

164
00:20:24,990 --> 00:20:27,170
 and included his own solutions to many problems.

165
00:20:27,350 --> 00:20:31,770
 All this greatly pleased the Emperor, who treated Marco as a great favorite.

166
00:20:33,150 --> 00:20:35,950
 Some believe in Jesus, others in Muhammad, still others in Buddha.

167
00:20:36,110 --> 00:20:38,490
 But I ask protection from all of them, to be sure.

168
00:20:39,510 --> 00:20:41,550
 Now here's your newest mission.

169
00:20:44,460 --> 00:20:44,640
 Here.

170
00:20:45,190 --> 00:20:46,530
 That's your orders, Great Kubla.

171
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:50,000
 Missions came and went, and so did the years.

172
00:20:58,450 --> 00:21:05,700
 After 17 years of service to the Great Kubla, our friends wanted to go home.

173
00:21:05,930 --> 00:21:08,490
 But the Great Khan was too fond of them to let them go.

174
00:21:11,390 --> 00:21:15,690
 Just when they were giving up all hope of seeing their homeland again,

175
00:21:16,050 --> 00:21:18,530
 three barons from faraway Persia arrived at the court.

176
00:21:18,530 --> 00:21:22,750
 They requested the emperor to provide their lord with a new wife, his own having died.

177
00:21:23,880 --> 00:21:28,930
 We beg of you, let the Polos accompany us home.

178
00:21:29,130 --> 00:21:30,590
 Seasoned travelers like them are rare.

179
00:21:30,670 --> 00:21:32,650
 You would enjoy a heartfelt, boundless gratitude.

180
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:38,000
 The emperor's great respect for his Western adventurers made his decision very difficult,

181
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:41,960
 but finally he gave in and requested the Polos to escort the barons and the princess.

182
00:21:42,690 --> 00:21:44,980
 Of course, he had to find himself a new set of advisors.

183
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:50,300
 Oh, yes, there is.

184
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:58,130
 In the great clan, our friends received safe conducts, messages for the Christian kings, and...

185
00:21:58,130 --> 00:22:00,910
 and 13 ships to take them safely home.

186
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:06,760
 In view of the trip they were about to make, 13 was none too many.

187
00:22:15,080 --> 00:22:18,100
 The return trip took three years, and judging by the looks of our friends,

188
00:22:18,450 --> 00:22:21,740
 we can suppose that it had been no stroll down the Primrose Path.

189
00:22:30,830 --> 00:22:41,580
 Come in, but you'll have to sleep in the stable.

190
00:22:43,360 --> 00:22:46,740
 The next day, there was a family reunion.

191
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:58,240
 Hallows had been away from home for 24 years.

192
00:22:58,740 --> 00:23:00,060
 Palace of the Great Calm.

193
00:23:00,430 --> 00:23:03,410
 Its hall is so vast that 6,000 people could eat there easily.

194
00:23:03,410 --> 00:23:07,430
 It has too many rooms, even to count.

195
00:23:08,510 --> 00:23:11,370
 All is so, so big and so grand, it leaves me speechless.

196
00:23:11,490 --> 00:23:12,390
 I can't describe it.

197
00:23:12,590 --> 00:23:14,970
 It's so lovely, no one would ever build a better world.

198
00:23:15,230 --> 00:23:16,150
 Well, what do you know?

199
00:23:19,210 --> 00:23:25,080
 Four in the sick, old persons and orphans are lodged by the great con and every day hot bread is passed out.

200
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:37,080
 In the province of Zandan Dan, when a woman gives birth, she gets right up out of her bed and goes straight to work.

201
00:23:38,290 --> 00:23:44,860
 Hubby gets in bed instead, takes the baby and lies there up to 40 days while all his relatives and friends fill his ears with praise.

202
00:23:46,350 --> 00:23:47,880
 Come on, that's nonsense.

203
00:23:48,180 --> 00:23:50,900
 No, it's not, but there's more. Where do you hear this one, folks?

204
00:23:50,900 --> 00:23:55,590
 The Lord's men harvest great quantities of pith from mulberry trees.

205
00:23:55,710 --> 00:23:57,330
 It's found just beneath the bark of the tree.

206
00:23:58,840 --> 00:24:01,300
 From this is made a paper, dark as ink, which they cut up.

207
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:03,680
 Even a scrap of the stuff's worth a half a denure.

208
00:24:04,020 --> 00:24:07,600
 A big sheet is worth one bazant in gold and so on and so on, up to ten bazants.

209
00:24:08,060 --> 00:24:10,230
 On these papers you see the senor's seal.

210
00:24:10,590 --> 00:24:13,990
 He's already made such a huge quantity of this stuff he could cash in all the world's gems.

211
00:24:14,130 --> 00:24:15,430
 Mark my words, but not just gems.

212
00:24:15,670 --> 00:24:18,050
 He could swallow up entire kingdoms if he should ever want to.

213
00:24:18,330 --> 00:24:21,100
 You might not believe what I'm about to say, but you just wait.

214
00:24:21,180 --> 00:24:23,840
 He'll buy out the planet one fine day and pay in cash.

215
00:24:25,110 --> 00:24:28,550
 In his capital alone are piled the most massive treasures I've seen anywhere in the world.

216
00:24:28,870 --> 00:24:31,470
 Each day over a thousand cartloads of fine silks pour in.

217
00:24:31,590 --> 00:24:35,270
 And so, Peking's thousands of inhabitants are quite used to dressing in pure silk.

218
00:24:35,650 --> 00:24:37,710
 This fellow exaggerates, folks.

219
00:24:38,340 --> 00:24:42,260
 Throughout the province of Cathay, there exists a kind of black stone found in the mountains.

220
00:24:42,760 --> 00:24:45,020
 These amazing black stones burn brightly when lit.

221
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:46,780
 You can decide the light of fire in the evening.

222
00:24:47,080 --> 00:24:48,180
 You'll feel comfy all night.

223
00:24:48,340 --> 00:24:50,140
 The next day, the black stones still give off heat.

224
00:24:50,140 --> 00:25:00,840
 no less than 15 000 boats sail up and down the world's longest river it flows through hang chow

225
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:06,100
 an immense city in which you'd be amazed to see 12 000 bridges so high ships of great height may

226
00:25:06,100 --> 00:25:10,500
 glide beneath them i tell you that venice's loftiest ship and full sail could easily pass

227
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:14,480
 mast intact and what is even more interesting is the city was planned and built on water

228
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:27,650
 1298, Venice is at war with Genoa, its rival city, near the island of Krizola on a Venetian ship.

229
00:25:27,970 --> 00:25:30,520
 There are quite a few and well-armed. This could be a rough battle.

230
00:25:30,980 --> 00:25:32,760
 To your positions, prepare for combat!

231
00:25:34,460 --> 00:25:38,530
 Our friends put up a good fight, but...

232
00:25:38,530 --> 00:25:41,740
 Retreat! Run for your lives!

233
00:25:43,210 --> 00:25:45,610
 We won't be long, Michael. Courage!

234
00:25:45,930 --> 00:25:56,160
 No man, be he Christian, Saracen, Tartar or pagan, had ever set foot in so many lands as did our brave, distinguished friend Marco Polo.

235
00:25:56,160 --> 00:26:06,330
 In spite of a few minor mistakes and myths, he gave the Western world its first realistic description of the Far East, which up until his time had been unknown.

236
00:26:06,330 --> 00:26:12,320
 Two centuries later, Christopher Columbus sailed westward in search of Cathay in the Indies described by Polo.

237
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:15,170
 What Columbus discovered instead were the Americas.

